            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Ovid</strong><br>\nPublius Ovidius Naso, known as Ovid, was born on March 20, 43 BC, in Sulmo, present-day Sulmona, in the Apennine mountains east of Rome. His family was important and wealthy, which allowed him to study in Rome, and later in Athens, to become a public speaker. However, he discovered his passion for poetry early on and decided to pursue it instead of a career in public service.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ovid's Literary Work</strong><br>\nOvid was a prolific Roman poet, whose writing was characterized by wit and eloquence. His early works include the \"Amores,\" poems about love, and the \"Heroides,\" fictional letters by legendary women to absent husbands. Ovid's work is often associated with the elegiac couplet, which he used to explore themes of love, myth, and metamorphosis with innovative narrative techniques.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"The Art of Love\"</strong><br>\nOne of Ovid's most famous works is the \"Ars Amatoria,\" or \"The Art of Love,\" a didactic poem offering advice on romance and seduction. Its humorous and often cheeky tone was unique for its time. This work provided detailed instructions for how to attract and keep a lover, which led to Ovid's immense popularity among Rome's elite social circles.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
