          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"Metamorphoses\" and Mythology</strong><br>\nThe \"Metamorphoses,\" Ovid's magnum opus, is an epic poem that chronicles the history of the world from its creation to the deification of Julius Caesar. It contains over 250 myths of transformation and is considered one of the most influential works of Western literature. Through vivid storytelling, Ovid explores the power of change and the human condition.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Art and Literature</strong><br>\nOvid's works have deeply influenced Western art and literature, inspiring countless artists, writers, and poets. His imaginative approach to myth and storytelling provided a rich source of inspiration for the Renaissance artists and continues to be reflected in modern literature, with his characters and stories serving as archetypal references and sources of human and philosophical insight.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Banishment to Tomis</strong><br>\nIn 8 AD, Emperor Augustus banished Ovid to Tomis, a remote town on the Black Sea, now Constanta in Romania. The reasons for his exile are not clear but are thought to be related to the scandalous nature of \"The Art of Love\" and possibly to Ovid's knowledge of a sensitive matter concerning the imperial family. Ovid's poetry from exile reflects his sorrow and longing for Rome.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
