          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Life in Exile</strong><br>\nWhile in exile, Ovid wrote two poetry collections, \"Tristia\" and \"Epistulae ex Ponto,\" which express his despair and the harsh conditions of his life far from home. He pleaded in his works for leniency and return to Rome, but his requests were never granted. Despite his hardship, he continued to write until his death, which showed his enduring commitment to his art.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ovid's Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nOvid died in 17 or 18 AD in Tomis, without returning to Rome. His work has endured for millennia, transcending the Roman Empire's fall and becoming integral to the European literary canon. Ovid's influence is seen in the works of Shakespeare, Dante, Chaucer, and many other literary figures, as well as in the ongoing study of classical myths and their reinterpretations.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Modern Reception of Ovid</strong><br>\nToday, Ovid is studied not just for his literary merit but also for his historical significance. Modern scholars and readers appreciate Ovid for his technical skill and the way he encapsulated the social and moral dynamics of his time. His exile is often viewed as a symbol of the artist's struggle against political oppression, making his life and work relevant to contemporary discussions on freedom of expression.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
